    "last_assistant_text": "For the fuzzer, the input is raw data in memory, so it probably uses `MemStream`. `MemStream::makeSubStream` creates a new `MemStream` sharing the same buffer. Each sub-stream has its own `bufPtr`, but they share the same `buf` pointer. Resetting one stream doesn't affect another's position.\n\nSo `constructXRef` calling `str->reset()` won't disrupt the parser's lexer position. Good.\n\nThe issue might be elsewhere. Let me think about what ACTUALLY happens during the `fetch(10, 0)` call inside `makeStream`'s `dictLookup`.\n\nWait — maybe the problem is that `constructXRef` calls `str->reset()` and then scans the ENTIRE file. This uses the SAME base stream that the parser's lexer is reading from. If the base stream is a MemStream, the sub-stream and the base stream share the same buffer but have independent positions. So resetting and scanning the base stream shouldn't affect the sub-stream.\n\nBut wait, in `XRef::fetch` at line 1098-1100:\n```c\nparser = new Parser(this,\n    new Lexer(this,\n        str->makeSubStream(start + e->offset, false, 0, Object(objNull))),\n    true);\n```\n\n`str` is the XRef's base stream. `makeSubStream` creates a sub-stream starting at `start + e->offset`. This sub-stream is unlimited (length=0 with limited=false). When `getObj` calls `makeStream`, the parser reads from this sub-stream.\n\nInside `makeStream`, `dict.dictLookup(\"Length\")` triggers `fetch(10, 0)`:\n1. `fetch(10, 0)` → err → `constructXRef`\n2. `constructXRef` uses `str` (XRef's base stream) to scan\n3. `constructXRef` calls `str->reset()` — resets the base stream\n4. `constructXRef` scans the file using `str->getLine(buf, 256)`\n5. After `constructXRef`, `str`'s position is somewhere in the file\n6. `fetch(10, 0, recursion+1)` → creates a NEW sub-stream for obj 10\n7. Returns the length value\n\nAfter step 7, control returns to `makeStream`. The parser's lexer still has its sub-stream (created in step at line 1098-1100), which should still be at the correct position (independent from the base stream).\n\nSo the stream parsing should continue normally after `dictLookup`. Let me try to verify this by creating a more carefully crafted PDF.\n\nActually, let me try something different. Let me look at whether `readXRefUntil` is called instead of `constructXRef` for the `getEntry` path. In `getEntry`:\n\n```c\nif (i >= size || entries[i].type == xrefEntryNone) {\n    if ((!xRefStream) && mainXRefEntriesOffset) {\n      // parseEntry path\n    } else {\n      readXRefUntil(i);\n      // check if i is in bounds...\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nFor a non-linearized PDF (mainXRefEntriesOffset=0), the `readXRefUntil` path is taken. `readXRefUntil` calls `readXRef` which calls `readXRefTable` or `readXRefStream`. This doesn't reallocate if the entry is found in a previous xref section.\n\nLet me focus specifically on `readXRefUntil` and see if it can trigger reallocation.",
